# Director-work Frame rules.
Begin every Frame with the exact required source sentence supplied by the Story Description, once and only once. Immediately prove the source relationship through concrete visible choices rather than repeating the visual summary.
Every Frame is a completely standalone image prompt with no textual dependency on another Frame. Fully restate the complete setting and every person's appearance, clothing or specific state of undress, image position, depth, orientation, pose, gaze, expression, and current interaction. Never use "the same person," "still," "earlier," "again," "continuing," "as before," or equivalent continuity shorthand.
Write one unbroken natural-language paragraph in this reading order: period, time, country, exact place, interior or exterior environment, foreground, middle ground, background, key objects, and motivated weather or atmospheric medium; exact cast count and each person's complete visible appearance, clothing or specific state of undress, image position, and depth; each person's orientation, held pose, limb actions, gaze, expression, spacing, occlusion, contact points, interaction, and physical support; composition, shot size, camera height, angle, lens perspective, focus, and depth of field; then motivated light sources, direction, hardness, color temperature, contrast, shadows, palette, tone, atmospheric light behavior, and concise image finish.
Do not print headings, numbered fields, aspect ratio, resolution, portrait or landscape orientation, or image dimensions. The App controls image geometry.
Use an exact focal length or aperture only when it explains a visible perspective, focus, or depth-of-field result. Do not list camera specifications for their own sake.
Preserve at least five concrete traits from the work-specific evidence across palette, composition, blocking, camera, light, production design, material, movement, and finish.
Never expose internal terms such as parent style, Theme style, aggregate style summary, work-set visual summary, visual profile, schema, prompt, or generation process.
Describe a small compatible set of visible finish qualities. Do not stack processing, print, scan, restoration, grain, damage, or artifact terminology.
